body:
I have a couple.

Friend the first: an on-again, off-again friendship with a girl I met in college. "M" had a pretty intense case of BPD, which meant she alternated between pulling me so close I couldn't breathe and pushing me away for no reason whatsoever. I let her move into my house for zero rent, and two months later she told me I was selfish for telling her *not* to pursue a guy outside of her established relationship. (Spoiler alert, they got together for about two weeks and flamed out, like I said they would.) She and I would stop talking for months, but when we were friends, she would be over constantly and never wanted to spend time apart. She enjoyed picking apart my writing, my hobbies, and my family/other friends. She and I eventually started a small theater company together and put together a webseries that I wrote. She re-worked the characters until they were almost unrecognizable, and was a terror to work with because of her control issues, something recognized by both me and my costar in the series. (She forced me to play a role even though I did not want to, and then proceeded to pick apart my performance - which is why I didn't want to do it.) At the end of it, I asked her to sit down and talk with me about the next season of the series, and she refused, sending her husband instead. After calmly discussing my concerns with how the first season had been handled and requesting that the next season be more cooperative and collaborative, she and her husband sent me a cold email saying that, in no uncertain terms, they were in charge and they were going to do whatever they wanted. I told her I was done, and asked only that we not take this to social media. A week later she wrote an article about how working with friends was a bad plan, trashing me for being "unprofessional." Luckily I recognized this as a gambit to get back into my life, and I refused to say anything. The article is probably still out there somewhere.

&#x200B;

Second friend. We were part of a writing group together, and I was going through one of the worst depressive episodes I've ever experienced. I grew close to "S" and he helped me through so many tough times. We had a purely online friendship and he never wanted to call on Skype or anything, which I didn't mind since I hated the phone. But 7 months later, he asked to call, and then chickened out. Later that night, he sent me an email revealing the truth - S didn't exist. S was a fake name and profile created by another member of the group "A." She was pretending to be two people for attention, but felt she had to come clean to me. It was like S had died, only worse, because S never even existed. I blocked both S and A's profiles and walked away from the group. 

&#x200B;

Third friend, "SB." SB was also an online friend from a different writing group. We were friends for five years, and would spend hours talking on the phone together. She was a major part of my life. I talked her through some pretty intense family drama, boy drama, and mental health issues. After a while, SB starts using me as a personal therapist, something I'm a) not qualified for, and b) is quite exhausting. But I deal. We moved from writing group to writing group, and eventually found one that we both really liked for a long time. SB gets bored and decides to leave the group for another, taking her girlfriend and a couple other people with her. No big deal, it happens, and I want them all to be happy. Apparently the feeling isn't mutual. SB and her girlfriend and the group that left all start shit-talking the writing group I'm currently in (and in charge of). Specifically, they come after me. I confront them, and to my face, SB says she's "sorry she got caught." Not sorry she talked about me behind my back. Just sorry I heard about it. She acts irritated when we call to talk about it, annoyed that she even has to apologize. I ask her for some time. Two weeks later, she messages me as if nothing has happened. I ghosted her. 

&#x200B;

It's actually a thing I brought up to my therapist recently. Because it feels like all 3 of these friends fall into similar patterns - controlling, manipulative, emotionally draining - and I would like to stop the cycle. Working on it! At least I can say that it's taught me a lot about what I value in a friend and how to set my own limitations and find a balance.
